INSERT INTO comments (post_id, name, email, text, status, spam_score, spam_reason, created_at) SELECT id, '@victornzomo', '', 'Timely piece. Allow me to quickly share a few comments and questions.

Comments:

1. There''s a clear balance which must be struck between film development and regulation of the film industry.

2. With reference to your "Constitutional right to freedom of choice" argument, I disagree. Even where the actions of KFCB are argued to be a limitation of fundamental freedoms in Articles 32, 33 and 34 of the Constitution, such limitation appears to be justifiable under Article 24 of the Constitution.

3. I dont agree with your contention that film bans necessarily stifle creativity. In this regard, I submit to you the case of graffitti which is by definition a criminal offence but nonetheless a popular recognised form of art. Also, the act of banning applies after the work has already been made and published. I do concede that the act of banning may however have an impact on creators'' proceeds from their works, although this is also arguable given the forbidden fruit effect.

Questions:

1. Can the decision to ban by KFCB be challenged through judicial review as unfair administration action as per Article 47 of the Constitution?
2. If 1 is true, who would have locus standi to challenge such a decision? A film buff, like me? Or Hollywood and/or its representatives?', 'approved', 0, 'imported from WordPress', '2014-01-17T10:43:02Z' FROM posts WHERE slug = 'film-censorship-and-bans-the-role-of-kenya-film-classification-board';
INSERT INTO comments (post_id, name, email, text, status, spam_score, spam_reason, created_at) SELECT id, 'Clement Paul', '', 'The Kenya Film Classification Board has got it wrong on this one.
My view is that their approach is more of REACTIONAL than PROACTIVE.
Kenya has come of age and due to technology society is changing each and everyday.
People have become more informed especially as regards media and what goes on globally in the entertainment world.
The film THE WOLF OF WALL STREET is just one among many other movies that shows nudity,use of drugs and violence and I think its ironic that this be the first film to be banned just because it has such themes.
If people havent noticed,most programmes being broadcasted today are based on real life events which may involve nudity,drugs and violence.For example,I know many soap operas such Bold and Beautiful and Young and the Restless which show worse themes like INCEST and yet they havent been banned when they used to air on television. This is not to condone such acts with in society but act as an agent to the realization on outcomes that have helped shape what society is today.
I also would like to also state something that KFCB should realize,Kenyans dont leave in the dark ages and if they do ban a film,it adds more fuel for people to go look for the movie on line in the internet.I think KFCB should stick to their madate of classifying and categorizing programmes instead of using "a hammer to kill a mosquito'''' approach in carrying out its madate.', 'approved', 0, 'imported from WordPress', '2014-01-17T10:46:12Z' FROM posts WHERE slug = 'film-censorship-and-bans-the-role-of-kenya-film-classification-board';
INSERT INTO comments (post_id, name, email, text, status, spam_score, spam_reason, created_at) SELECT id, 'gakuria said', '', 'All movie enthusiasts like me have already watched it.the movie is already doing heavy rotation.gava is grapling feathers...chicken is long gone', 'approved', 0, 'imported from WordPress', '2014-01-17T10:46:37Z' FROM posts WHERE slug = 'film-censorship-and-bans-the-role-of-kenya-film-classification-board';
INSERT INTO comments (post_id, name, email, text, status, spam_score, spam_reason, created_at) SELECT id, 'Miss Roses', '', 'What do I say? This is a very informative post. I knew all the mandated of the KFC as I have done a couple of stories on them in the past.

Now as for the Kenya Film Classification Board, I don''t really get the reasoning behind the directive. Like I said in my FB, there are many other films that have used the F word a million times like Inglorious Bastards and were shown on big screen. The same way sex and drugs has been a theme in many move is and series, even Breaking Bad, ok even though it didn''t air locally. It''s just another adult movie with sadistic humour displaying the post jazz era in renewed profligacy. The excessive lifestyle of money, drugs and sex. That said, its not a movie for everyone''s taste, however it doesn''t deserve the ban. I mean it''s up for several Oscars and at the Golden Globes it was actually nominated for Comedy. this shows us that we have a long way to go in terms of liberalization of creativity, especially of local films.', 'approved', 0, 'imported from WordPress', '2014-01-17T10:57:23Z' FROM posts WHERE slug = 'film-censorship-and-bans-the-role-of-kenya-film-classification-board';
INSERT INTO comments (post_id, name, email, text, status, spam_score, spam_reason, created_at) SELECT id, 'Liz Lenjo', '', '1. In this 21st Century Victor, there is a thin line between regulating and dictating. And looking at the context of film development, how regulation is undertaken influences the trends especially in our developing nation.
2. In so far as creativity is concerned, it is important that imaginative re-creations, SCI-FI and other unrealistic stylistic devices employed to create a motion picture are separated from the reality. I think there is a mix in both at the moment and a difficulty to separate rather obvious.
3. Like I said, it is my humble opinion. In addition, such bans are definitely do not favour our industry players to try something new. I stand corrected but has there been a Kenyan Horror Film released since Otto was banned? Have you not noticed the monotony that is Kenyan TV  because what is outside the box in our society is not easily accepted?And why?Because KFCB exercising such bans on local films discourages the creators in the industry. 

Answer to your questions:
1. In regards to a local film I believe yes, KFCB can and should be challenged through Judicial review. Freedom of expression should be respected. I wish Joseph Kinuthia, the Director of Otto has challenged the KFCB then. I do not understand why the double standards. We also need to accept that times have changed. We are not operating as Islanders. The more we are exposed to the world and its different cultures, the more we assimilate and our values get influenced and change. 

In the USA, there are cases where the aggrieved parties have litigated to exercise their Constitutional rights. Allow me to share with you a list of banned movies in the US and their developments.  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_films_banned_in_the_United_States

Regarding this ban and other international movies that have been banned and who can challenged KFCB through JR that is a good question.May be if Civil Society still existed and their was one with a particular bias in this. May be.', 'approved', 0, 'imported from WordPress', '2014-01-17T11:09:05Z' FROM posts WHERE slug = 'film-censorship-and-bans-the-role-of-kenya-film-classification-board';
INSERT INTO comments (post_id, name, email, text, status, spam_score, spam_reason, created_at) SELECT id, 'Liz Lenjo', '', 'I totally agree. We need to stop with this double standard. In my opinion, I think it is possible that KFCB is struggling for relevance. What have we not been exposed to in this day and age? People have access to pornography which is way more explicit than this film.', 'approved', 0, 'imported from WordPress', '2014-01-17T11:13:17Z' FROM posts WHERE slug = 'film-censorship-and-bans-the-role-of-kenya-film-classification-board';
