ring it closer home with an analogy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If a random stranger came to one of your many houses and decided to paint it, would you accept it even if it’s free? Or they come to plough your "},{"type":"text","text":"shamba","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and grow sweet potatoes without your permission, would you be okay with that because it’s free? You would probably call the police on them, right? Intellectual property rights are proprietary rights. As I always like to say time and time again, one’s Intellectual Property is their "},{"type":"text","text":"Shamba","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"There is no such thing as infringement free copying sans permission of the rights holder. It does not help the situation when those who do not have the professional knowledge give bogus advice. Have you heard of Abass Kubaff’s \"Moral Dilemma\" where he samples the original recording of Adele’s \"Hello\" with the help of a DJ he collaborated with? When the song hit the airwaves, I heard some radio hosts praise how awesome it was and that they hoped that he paid royalties. The IP lawyer in me scoffed at that statement with rage. What does “pay royalties” mean in this context exactly?"}]},{"type":"figure","attrs":{"key":"wp-content/uploads/2015/12/band-984164_960_720.jpg","src":null,"alt":"band-984164_960_720","caption":""}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ideally when and if you want to use someone else’s song, you MUST seek consent. These are their rights and they have authority to deny permission, sue for unauthorized use and do as they please with their musical property. If you want to obtain rights clearance over use of a song, an artist needs to contact the copyright holder who is usually the author and/or composer. The research in this process is intense and due diligence must be precise because it will not matter if you paid the wrong person or entity. Where the composer/author has assigned their rights to a music publisher, you then deal with the publisher and not the author/composer. In my opinion, the most organized composers/ authors have publishers to undertake the burden of making sure they make money from their music."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Different publishing houses have different practices. Some of the legal infrastructure these publishers have include sampling licenses at a fee, Mechanical Rights Agreements also at a fee, Adaptation licenses at a fee. Whatever use you envision in your artistic mind, you must seek consent and get the necessary licenses and pay the required fees where stipulated. There may be instances where you may be lucky to get a free consent, and if you do, it must be in writing. If it is not, well, let’s just say… it is not free. It’s what is provided under the law. All licenses must be in writing where copyright is concerned. A “verbal license” will not suffice. In summary, where one fails to get consent/ a license to use another’s musical works, they are liable for copyright infringement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"When these rights holders are granting the licenses, they reserve the right to hear the final product, to question the use for example; what part of the song you are sampling, the time length of the sampling, the artists involved in the re-recording of their work and any other material information they find necessary. All these factors contribute in how much you would pay to the rights holders. For example in Adele’s instance, Dela and Abass (if he did not) should have sought consent from "},{"type":"text","text":"Universal Music Group- UK","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"http://www.umusicpub.co.uk/en-GB/Artists/A/Adele.aspx"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for further direction before proceeding to record."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I am aware there are a few Kenyan artists who do ensure they clear the rights appropriately and in the right manner as required under the copyright laws. For example, Kevin Wyre, The Love Child, obtained the Mechanical rights to use “"},{"type":"text","text":"Sina Makosa","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":"” from the recognized rightful owner at the time he was re-recording the song."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perhaps, our entertainment journalists should also highlight some of these crucial information when interviewing artists so that others learn from those who do right by other copyright holders."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Take home points?"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":" You MUST research to find out who has the rights or control over the song."},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":" You MUST seek their CONSENT/LICENSE."},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":" You MUST get a LICENSE."},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":" You MUST have the consent/ license in WRITING."},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":" You MUST pay for the License / Consent as stipulated by the Rights Holder."},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":" You CAN negotiate. After all licenses are negotiations."},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":" Even music made for free distribution must be sought for a license/ consent first from the owner."},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":" If you have not sought consent and the rights holder is silent, it does not mean you are not liable for copyright infringement. Your turn to be caught is coming though."},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":" If you are sued, be ready to pay lots and lots of money. Far more than what you would have paid for the license had you sought it in the beginning. Have you read the case against Robin Thicke for the "},{"type":"text","text":"infringement","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"http://pitchfork.com/news/58743-jury-rules-pharrell-and-robin-thickes-blurred-lines-copied-marvin-gaye/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" on Marvin Gaye’s song?"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":" YES, our Copyright Laws protect International Rights Holders as well. (I have heard several comments that international music is free. No it is not!)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Photos: Courtesy of "},{"type":"text","text":"Pixabay","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://pixabay.com/en/microphone-music-studio-1003559/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Images","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://pixabay.com/static/uploads/photo/2015/10/12/15/05/band-984164_960_720.jpg"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}', 'This has been an interesting year for Kenyan music. We have had some great hits and some serious misses. I have been meaning to tackle the issue of seeking consent since earlier in the year when DJ Pierra released the song Wape a re-arrangement of Queen of Taarab Bi. Malika. Now, here we are and Dela has done a wonderful cover of Adele’s "Hello". According to an article by All Africa, they spoke to Dela and the article made a declaration that a cover for Youtube purposes does not amount to copyright infringement. This is a very laughable conclusion. So, I shall bring it closer home with an analogy.
If a random stranger came to one of your many houses and decided to paint it, would you accept it even if it’s free? Or they come to plough your shamba and grow sweet potatoes without your permission, would you be okay with that because it’s free? You would probably call the police on them, right? Intellectual property rights are proprietary rights. As I always like to say time and time again, one’s Intellectual Property is their Shamba.
There is no such thing as infringement free copying sans permission of the rights holder. It does not help the situation when those who do not have the professional knowledge give bogus advice. Have you heard of Abass Kubaff’s "Moral Dilemma" where he samples the original recording of Adele’s "Hello" with the help of a DJ he collaborated with? When the song hit the airwaves, I heard some radio hosts praise how awesome it was and that they hoped that he paid royalties. The IP lawyer in me scoffed at that statement with rage. What does “pay royalties” mean in this context exactly?
Ideally when and if you want to use someone else’s song, you MUST seek consent. These are their rights and they have authority to deny permission, sue for unauthorized use and do as they please with their musical property. If you want to obtain rights clearance over use of a song, an artist needs to contact the copyright holder who is usually the author and/or composer. The research in this process is intense and due diligence must be precise because it will not matter if you paid the wrong person or entity. Where the composer/author has assigned their rights to a music publisher, you then deal with the publisher and not the author/composer. In my opinion, the most organized composers/ authors have publishers to undertake the burden of making sure they make money from their music.
Different publishing houses have different practices. Some of the legal infrastructure these publishers have include sampling licenses at a fee, Mechanical Rights Agreements also at a fee, Adaptation licenses at a fee. Whatever use you envision in your artistic mind, you must seek consent and get the necessary licenses and pay the required fees where stipulated. There may be instances where you may be lucky to get a free consent, and if you do, it must be in writing. If it is not, well, let’s just say… it is not free. It’s what is provided under the law. All licenses must be in writing where copyright is concerned. A “verbal license” will not suffice. In summary, where one fails to get consent/ a license to use another’s musical works, they are liable for copyright infringement.
When these rights holders are granting the licenses, they reserve the right to hear the final product, to question the use for example; what part of the song you are sampling, the time length of the sampling, the artists involved in the re-recording of their work and any other material information they find necessary. All these factors contribute in how much you would pay to the rights holders. For example in Adele’s instance, Dela and Abass (if he did not) should have sought consent from Universal Music Group- UK for further direction before proceeding to record.
I am aware there are a few Kenyan artists who do ensure they clear the rights appropriately and in the right manner as required under the copyright laws. For example, Kevin Wyre, The Love Child, obtained the Mechanical rights to use “Sina Makosa” from the recognized rightful owner at the time he was re-recording the song.
Perhaps, our entertainment journalists should also highlight some of these crucial information when interviewing artists so that others learn from those who do right by other copyright holders.
Take home points?
 You MUST research to find out who has the rights or control over the song.
 You MUST seek their CONSENT/LICENSE.
 You MUST get a LICENSE.
 You MUST have the consent/ license in WRITING.
 You MUST pay for the License / Consent as stipulated by the Rights Holder.
 You CAN negotiate. After all licenses are negotiations.
 Even music made for free distribution must be sought for a license/ consent first from the owner.
 If you have not sought consent and the rights holder is silent, it does not mean you are not liable for copyright infringement. Your turn to be caught is coming though.
 If you are sued, be ready to pay lots and lots of money. Far more than what you would have paid for the license had you sought it in the beginning. Have you read the case against Robin Thicke for the infringement on Marvin Gaye’s song?
 YES, our Copyright Laws protect International Rights Holders as well. (I have heard several comments that international music is free. No it is not!)
Photos: Courtesy of Pixabay Images.', 922, 'wp-content/uploads/2015/12/music-studio-hd-wallpapers.jpg', '', 'published', '2015-12-14T09:30:23Z', '2015-12-14T09:30:23Z', '2015-12-14T09:53:18Z', 0)
